              receptors, to ensure ignition of distributed frontoparietal
              activity. Our model predicts, counterintuitively, that
              fast-responding sensory areas contain a higher ratio of NMDA to
              AMPA receptors compared to association cortical areas that show
              slow, sustained activity. We validate this prediction using
              cortex-widein-vitroreceptor autoradiography data. Finally, we show
              how this model can account for various behavioral and
              physiological effects linked to consciousness. Together, these
              findings clarify the neurophysiological mechanisms of conscious
              access in the primate cortex and support the concept that
