  journal   = "J. Child Psychol. Psychiatry",
  publisher = "Wiley",
  volume    =  51,
  number    =  9,
  pages     = "981--988",
  abstract  = "BACKGROUND: The 'broken mirror' theory of autism, which proposes
               that a dysfunction of the human mirror neuron system (MNS) is
               responsible for the core social and cognitive deficits in
               individuals with autism spectrum disorders (ASD), has received
               considerable attention despite weak empirical evidence. METHODS:
               In this electroencephalographic study, we examined mu
               suppression, as an indicator of sensorimotor resonance,
