  number    =  6012,
  pages     = "1830--1834",
  abstract  = "Mind Reading One core component of social cognition, especially
               of the kind practiced by humans, is the capacity to formulate a
               representation of what someone else believes to be true, even if
               that belief is not anchored in reality. Holding two such beliefs
               in mind—one false and one true—is no simple feat, and up until a
