  journal       = "arXiv [cs.CR]",
  abstract      = "Deep learning algorithms have been shown to perform extremely
                   well on many classical machine learning problems. However,
                   recent studies have shown that deep learning, like other
                   machine learning techniques, is vulnerable to adversarial
                   samples: inputs crafted to force a deep neural network (DNN)
                   to provide adversary-selected outputs. Such attacks can
                   seriously undermine the security of the system supported by
                   the DNN, sometimes with devastating consequences. For
                   example, autonomous vehicles can be crashed, illicit or
                   illegal content can bypass content filters, or biometric
                   authentication systems can be manipulated to allow improper
                   access. In this work, we introduce a defensive mechanism
                   called defensive distillation to reduce the effectiveness of
                   adversarial samples on DNNs. We analytically investigate the
