  volume    =  116,
  number    =  1,
  pages     = "42--55",
  abstract  = "Scalar inferences are commonly generated when a speaker uses a
               weaker expression rather than a stronger alternative, e.g., John
               ate some of the apples implies that he did not eat them all. This
               article describes a visual-world study investigating how and when
               perceivers compute these inferences. Participants followed spoken
               instructions containing the scalar quantifier some directing them
               to interact with one of several referential targets (e.g., Click
