  volume    =  50,
  number    =  5,
  pages     = "1864--1881",
  abstract  = "Manual classification is still a common method to evaluate event
               detection algorithms. The procedure is often as follows: Two or
               three human coders and the algorithm classify a significant
               quantity of data. In the gold standard approach, deviations from
               the human classifications are considered to be due to mistakes of
               the algorithm. However, little is known about human
               classification in eye tracking. To what extent do the
               classifications from a larger group of human coders agree? Twelve
               experienced but untrained human coders classified fixations in 6
               min of adult and infant eye-tracking data. When using the
               sample-based Cohen's kappa, the classifications of the humans
               agreed near perfectly. However, we found substantial differences
