               and number of fixations. We hypothesized that the human coders
               applied different (implicit) thresholds and selection rules.
               Indeed, when spatially close fixations were merged, most of the
               classification differences disappeared. On the basis of the
               nature of these intercoder differences, we concluded that
               fixation classification by experienced untrained human coders is
               not a gold standard. To bridge the gap between agreement measures
               (e.g., Cohen's kappa) and eye movement parameters (fixation
               duration, number of fixations), we suggest the use of the
               event-based F1 score and two new measures: the relative timing
