  volume    =  17,
  pages     =  1358844,
  abstract  = "The zebrafish (Danio rerio) is increasingly used in neuroscience
               research. Zebrafish are relatively easy to maintain, and their
               high fecundity makes them suitable for high-throughput
               experiments. Their small, transparent embryos and larvae allow
               for easy microscopic imaging of the developing brain. Zebrafish
               also share a high degree of genetic similarity with humans, and
               are amenable to genetic manipulation techniques, such as gene
               knockdown, knockout, or knock-in, which allows researchers to
               study the role of specific genes relevant to human brain
               development, function, and disease. Zebrafish can also serve as a
               model for behavioral studies, including locomotion, learning, and
               social interactions. In this review, we present state-of-the-art
