  volume    =  3,
  number    =  7,
  pages     = "pgae264",
  abstract  = "Collective motion provides a spectacular example of
               self-organization in Nature. Visual information plays a crucial
               role among various types of information in determining
               interactions. Recently, experiments have revealed that organisms
               such as fish and insects selectively utilize a portion, rather
               than the entirety, of visual information. Here, focusing on fish,
               we propose an agent-based model where the direction of attention
               is guided by visual stimuli received from the images of nearby
               fish. Our model reproduces a branching phenomenon where a fish
               selectively follows a specific individual as the distance between
               two or three nearby fish increases. Furthermore, our model
               replicates various patterns of collective motion in a group of
               agents, such as vortex, polarized school, swarm, and turning. We
               also discuss the topological nature of the visual interaction, as
               well as the positional distribution of nearby fish and the map of
               pairwise and three-body interactions induced by them. Through a
